{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe overestimation bias: The model consistently forecasts a slow, monotonic increase or plateau (e.g., 90+ or 84+), failing to capture the actual sharp mean reversion and volatility (drops to 76-78) observed in the true data.",
    "Apply aggressive downward correction when sentiment history shows a divergence: If recent sentiment is high (1.00) but the price history shows a recent peak followed by a drop (e.g., 90.16 -> 85.58), the model's inertia causes it to ignore the reversal; reduce forecasts by 5-10 EUR immediately.",
    "Account for 'lagged' volatility: The model fails to react to the 10-15 EUR price swings seen in the true outcomes; forecasts must be adjusted to reflect the actual range (76-92) rather than the model's narrow band (83-92).",
    "Invert the trend assumption: When the 18-day history ends with a decline (e.g., 90.16 to 83.19), the model incorrectly assumes a continuation of the prior uptrend; force a downward correction for the first 10-15 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "Sentiment-weighted mean reversion: High sentiment scores (1.00) in the history correlate with the model's failure to predict the subsequent crash; treat high sentiment as a contrarian signal for an impending drop rather than a confirmation of strength."
  ]
}
